// @check
// @error `as%` wraps between integer widths, and f64 is a float — use the exact family (`as!` traps, `as?` yields null)
// @error `as%` wraps between integer widths, and f32 is a float — use the exact family (`as!` traps, `as?` yields null)
// @error `as%` wraps between integer widths, and f64 is a float — use the exact family (`as!` traps, `as?` yields null)
// @error `as` supports numeric conversions only
// @error `as` supports numeric conversions only
// @error `as` supports numeric conversions only
// @error unknown type `Bogus` in `as` cast
// @hint redundant type annotation: `ff64` is inferred as `f64`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `ff32` is inferred as `f32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `fi32` is inferred as `i32`
// What `x as% T` refuses, and why each refusal is the one its neighbour in the family gives.
//
//   · A FLOAT on either side. Wrapping is a statement about a bit pattern in a fixed width,
//     and a float has neither. The refusal names the family that does convert a float, so the
//     author is one character from the cast they meant.
//   · A NON-NUMERIC operand — a string, a struct, a union. `as%` picks no arm, so a union is
//     simply not a number here and takes the same sentence a string does. Two casts say what
//     one cannot: `(u as! i32) as% u8`.
//   · `as% Bogus` is the CONTROL: an unresolvable target keeps its own wording, so the float
//     rule is not swallowing an ordinary unknown type.
function ff64(x: f64): f64 { x }
function ff32(x: f32): f32 { x }
function fi32(x: i32): i32 { x }

print(ff64(3.9) as% i32)
print(ff32(3.5) as% i32)
print(fi32(5) as% f64)

const s = "x"
print(s as% i32)

const u: i32 | string = 5
print(u as% i32)

function fromHole(a) { a as% i32 }
print(fromHole(u))

print(fi32(5) as% Bogus)
